#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e1b9c8-c172-55e9-a193-1566e7a176ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈5,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307807} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe8c2f0-7259-511c-9a9c-616965f71a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307808} {:answer/id #uuid "69827cbf-91bd-51c1-a18c-88bd58099562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307809} {:answer/id #uuid "0773a6ad-3ae3-5314-ac4a-f5fb07337991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307810} {:answer/id #uuid "5fed7cee-9478-5fdd-a8b3-e5c9c332d567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈5,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69827cbf-91bd-51c1-a18c-88bd58099562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307809} :answer-field/id #uuid "32d91e4c-7e0e-5208-a76a-7c62ab53938d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7b7e632-32b9-5dd9-98c7-3b49b8d1e749" :question/math-academy-id "q-115348" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ estimate the value of $f(\\sqrt{2})$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(0,5),$ so we have $f(0)=5.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =5+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x-0) \\\\  & =5+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(\\sqrt{2})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{2}) & ≈L(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =5+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =5+1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an overestimate."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f45327ce-b54d-586f-b76a-f2776bcc3c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307814} {:answer/id #uuid "8174112b-33f7-56f9-b489-a475ee2f5365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307815} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec6a471-146b-51c6-927c-e790b919f703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307816} {:answer/id #uuid "ba26e40a-b1fc-5f4f-a0b4-a373198ab528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307817} {:answer/id #uuid "22e8d09a-be57-592f-84a0-f0428b352ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22e8d09a-be57-592f-84a0-f0428b352ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307818} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f9c42fe-7ed4-530a-b364-e83a670e6de4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f9a4e20-7e8e-527a-be2f-a1754c63ae1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115592" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1)$ and $f(1)={e}^{2}.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{3}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)={e}^{2},$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1),$ then ${f}^{′}(1)=2{e}^{2\\cdot 1}\\cdot 1(1+1)=4{e}^{2}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{3}{4})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b854078b-47db-5193-a0cd-d42ad69efbef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307821} {:answer/id #uuid "c244da4d-5e53-533e-8d9e-8ce759941e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b854078b-47db-5193-a0cd-d42ad69efbef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307821} :answer-field/id #uuid "9da86ea4-5c69-52a6-b902-4f11428992f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69c1051a-7247-5166-be7e-e99dad87d408" :question/math-academy-id "q-314897" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(5x+1)$ and $f(1)=3.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(1.3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(1.3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)=3,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(5x+1),$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)=(1)^{2}(5\\cdot 1+1)=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=3+6(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.3) & ≈L(1.3) \\\\  & =3+6(1.3-1) \\\\  & =3+6(0.3) \\\\  & =3+1.8 \\\\  & =4.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c8eda61-ccef-51be-8254-c2389f08a078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307825} {:answer/id #uuid "cf186a0a-ddd5-5f38-8d13-e848700772bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307826} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbca01d-3d19-57cb-9035-573112a763fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307827} {:answer/id #uuid "c47e9b14-7599-5812-8de5-05962646251c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307828} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1d880b-4fa1-5b40-b6cc-b7c458918701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c47e9b14-7599-5812-8de5-05962646251c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307828} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9b2b8ca-0231-545a-b9eb-2c5ff9a7ab1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6301d818-81c8-590c-bd65-3b50215e3aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307831} {:answer/id #uuid "540f5e8d-cf38-5703-af3f-60c0bbb8345c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "540f5e8d-cf38-5703-af3f-60c0bbb8345c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307832} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ab7598-13fc-558b-b522-0ae6748fadf1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6564027c-6cd6-5448-ad69-9b0166d7b931" :question/math-academy-id "q-314901" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(2,3)$ and is concave up on the interval $(1,4).$ If ${f}^{′}(2)=1.5,$ estimate the value of $f(1.7)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.\n$\\,f(1.7)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(2,3),$ so we have $f(2)=3.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(2)=1.5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+1.5(x-2) \\\\  & =3+1.5x-3 \\\\  & =1.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.7)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.7.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.7) & ≈L(1.7) \\\\  & =1.5(1.7) \\\\  & =2.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(1,4),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph near $x=2,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{underestimate}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "265476d0-340b-59f4-b37e-85a8dd13e573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "265476d0-340b-59f4-b37e-85a8dd13e573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307835} :answer-field/id #uuid "668d4876-cd33-5e2a-bd18-6361ca4688a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37e5380a-ba8f-520a-aa7a-394a026c36ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-346054" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{3}-1$ and $f(-1)=2.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ to estimate the value of $f(-0.6).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(-0.6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(-1)=2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{3}-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)=(-1)^{3}-1=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=2-2(x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(-0.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-0.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.6) & ≈L(-0.6) \\\\  & =2-2(-0.6+1) \\\\  & =2-2(0.4) \\\\  & =2-0.8 \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
